  "summary": "Explored three approaches for human-gated approval: (1) A dedicated 'final-human-approval' checkpoint phase inserted between 'approved' and 'spec-writing', triggered by a per-item flag like `\"requires_human_approval\": true` in the feature JSON. The pipeline's auto-advance logic in phases.py would check this flag after plan review passes and route flagged items to a hold phase instead of proceeding directly. This is the simplest approach and reuses the existing manual-move patterns already present in the WebUI/TUI. (2) A board-level config option in .mad/config.json (e.g., `\"human_gate_before_impl\": true` or a list of item types/tags that require gating) so entire boards or categories of work can be gated without per-item configuration. This could combine with approach 1 — board config sets the default, per-item flag overrides. (3) A tag/label-based system where items tagged with something like `needs-human-review` are automatically held at configurable phase boundaries. This is the most flexible but adds complexity. Recommendation: Start with approach 1 (per-item boolean flag) combined with approach 2 (board-level default). Add a `requires_human_approval` field to the feature JSON schema, default it based on board config, and check it in the pipeline transition between plan-review-pass and spec-writing. The existing `final-human-approval` phase and WebUI move buttons already provide the UI for humans to release items. A new phase name like `awaiting-human-approval` (distinct from `final-human-approval` which is post-implementation) would make the workflow clearer. The pipeline change is minimal: in phases.py after plan review PASS, check `feature.data.get('requires_human_approval')` and if true, move to `awaiting-human-approval` instead of `approved`. The WebUI already supports moving items between phases, so the human can move it to `approved` when ready."
